 nja Thiel and Elena Yu\, in dialogue with the France&#8211\;Berkeley resea
 rch initiative The Latent Spaces of Culture\, and on display at the Worth 
 Ryder Art Gallery.In neural networks\, the "hidden layers" are the interme
 diate strata of transformation between input and output — the computatio
 nal structures where data are transformed\, weighted\, and recombined into
  increasingly abstract\, distributed representations that no human eye can
  read. The exhibition Hidden Layers:&#160\;AI Infrastructures and Hallucin
 ations takes this term as its conceptual frame\, bringing together sevente
 en international artists and researchers working at the intersection of co
 ntemporary art and machine learning to make visible what artificial intell
 igence keeps out of sight: its planetary infrastructures and computational
  latent spaces\, its colonial and ideological underpinnings\, its misrecog
 nitions and hallucinations\, and its transformation of our relation to the
  past through the silent reprocessing of pre-existing cultural forms. The 
 exhibition is organized in four sections: "In the Latent Spaces of Culture
 \," "Material Infrastructures\," "Colonial Imaginaries: From Misrecognitio
 n to Generative Slopaganda\," and "Hallucinations and Psychedelia."Featuri
 ng works by Nouf Aljowaysir (https://www.noufaljowaysir.com/)\; Gašper an
 d Nina Beguš & Metahaven (https://latentspacecraft.antikythera.org/)\; Gr
 égory Chatonsky (https://chatonsky.net/)\; Kate Crawford & Vladan Joler (
 https://anatomyof.ai/)\; Asma Kazmi (https://www.asmakazmi.com/) & Jill Mi
 ller (https://www.jillmiller.net/)\; Andrea Khôra (https://www.andreakhor
 a.com/)\; Occitane Lacurie & Barnabé Sauvage (https://www.schirn.de/en/sc
 hirnmag/3-questions-for-occitane-lacurie-und-barnabe-sauvage/)\; Greg Niem
 eyer (https://www.gregniemeyer.com/)\; and Mario Santamaria (https://mario
 santamaria.net/) & Alex Saum-Pascual (https://www.alexsaum.com/).Hidden La
 yers is a collaboration between the Department of German and the Worth Ryd
 er Art Gallery at the Department of Art Practice. It is part of the France
 –Berkeley research initiative The Latent Spaces of Culture: a collaborat
 ion between UC Berkeley\, the Université Sorbonne Nouvelle in Paris\, and
  the French National Center for Scientific Research (CNRS).Exhibition Date
 s: September 3 - October 3\, 2026Opening Reception: Thursday\, September 3
 \, 4-6:30pm\, Worth Ryder Art GalleryArtist Talk\, Gregory Chatonsky "INFR
 ALLUCINATION": Thursday\, September 3\, 6:30-8:00pm\, AAPB 285Film Program
 \, Push Buttons and AI (https://bampfa.org/event/push-buttons-and-ai): Sep
 tember 9\, 7 pm\, BAMPFACuratorial Tour\, led by Antonio Somaini: Friday\,
  September 11\, 4:30pm\, Worth Ryder Art GalleryWorkshop on Latent Space o
 f Culture: September 9\, 2pm-4pm\; September 10/11\, 9:30 am-4pm on both d
 ays\, 470 StephensSalon on AI and Art: September 16\, 6pm– 9pm\, AI Futu
 res LabTour of AI: October 3 Reservations required. Information forthcomin
 gClosing day: October 3\, 12-3pm\, Worth Ryder Art GalleryAdditional event
 s\, details\, and registration links to be announced. Times subject to cha
 nge\, please also check the campus calendar (https://events.berkeley.edu/a
 rt).This exhibition is supported by the France-Berkeley Fund of the Instit
 ute of European Studies\, the Department of German and the Department of A
 rt Practice\, together with the Berkeley Center for New Media\, the Berkel
 ey Institute for Data Science (BIDS)\, Department of Rhetoric\; Antikyther
 a\, the Human Technology Futures (HTF)\, the Pamela P. Fong and Family Dis
 tinguished Chair in China Studies\, the Division of Arts & Humanities\, th
 e Townsend Center for the Humanities and the School of Information\, the T
 echnology Network of the Berkeley Economy and Society Initiative (BESI)\, 
 and the Berkeley Art\, Law\, and Finance Project\, hosted by the Berkeley 
 Center for Law and Business at Berkeley Law.&#160\;In conjunction with the
  exhibition\, additional programming is developed in collaboration with Be
 rkeley Art Museum and Pacific Film Archive (BAMPFA) and the Institute for 
 Contemporary Art San Jose.
